tel 1.0

base proscenium
import soundness

info   the prelude every Soundness module compiles against: its collections, markers and blessed names
detail
    _Proscenium_ is the default namespace of Soundness. Every module in the build is
    compiled with `-Yno-predef -Yimports:java.lang,proscenium`, so neither `scala.Predef`
    nor the `scala` package is imported implicitly; what a source file sees without an
    import is `java.lang`, this package, and nothing else. A consumer outside the build
    reaches the same names through `import soundness.*`, since everything here is
    re-exported to the umbrella (the collection companions as stable `val`s, so their
    members resolve through the forwarders).

    The package has three kinds of content.

    **The collections.** `List`, `Sequence`, `Set`, `Map`, `Ledger`, `Chain` and `Array`
    are *opaque* aliases of the standard library's structures — a linked list, a `Vector`,
    a hash set, a hash map, an insertion-ordered `VectorMap`, a `LazyList` and a JVM array
    — which cost nothing to wrap and expose none of the stdlib's members. Their companions
    hold construction (`apply`, `empty`, `from`, `fill`, `tabulate`, `range`), pattern
    support, macro lifting, and the greppable `stdlib` bridge; every other operation —
    `map`, `filter`, `fold`, `head`, `at`, `size`, `sort` — is a typeclass-driven
    extension defined in the libraries above (`murmuration`, `denominative`,
    `concordance`, `rudiments`), reached with `import soundness.*`. A total vocabulary
    replaces the partial one: `head` returns an `Optional` unless the receiver is proven
    `Populated`, and the operations whose cost belies their name (a `List`'s `size`, a
    `Chain`'s) are gated behind a named import from `dysasymptotics`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val numbers = List(3, 1, 2)                  // List[Int] & Populated: the arity is the proof
    numbers.head                                 // 3, total on a populated list
    numbers.map(_*2)                             // List(6, 2, 4)
    Map(t"a" -> 1).at(t"b")                      // Unset
    numbers.stdlib                               // scala.collection.immutable.List(3, 1, 2)
    List.from(scala.collection.immutable.List(1, 2))
    ```

    There is deliberately no implicit conversion between an opaque collection and its
    stdlib form in either direction: a conversion applies at member selection too and would
    silently restore the whole partial API. Crossing to a stdlib or Java API is written as
    `xs.stdlib` — a zero-copy view, and a greppable marker of every seam — and back with the
    companion's `from` or `.to(List)`. A varargs splice, `f(xs*)`, works on an opaque
    collection directly, through the `Spreadable` givens beside each collection. Do not use
    `stdlib` to dodge a cost gate: `xs.stdlib.length` hides exactly the cost
    `dysasymptotics.linearSize` exists to acknowledge; open the gate with a comment saying
    why the cost is acceptable, or restructure.

    **The markers.** `Populated` is a structural refinement recording a proof of
    non-emptiness (`List[Int] & Populated`), minted by the fixed-arity literal constructors
    and by rudiments' `occupied`; `Sized[n]` records a proof of length, minted by `fill`
    and `tabulate` with a literal count; and `Comparison` (`Less`, `Same`, `More`) is the
    result of every three-way comparison in Soundness, the currency of symbolism's
    `Comparable`.

    **The blessed names.** `proscenium.prelude.scala` re-exports from `scala` only what
    Soundness code needs without an import: the fundamental and primitive types, the infix
    `&`/`|`/`<:<`/`=:=`, `Option`/`Either` and the tuple types, `CanEqual`, `Conversion`,
    `PartialFunction`, `StringContext`, the numeric abstractions, the annotations, `Iterable`
    and (for now) `StringBuilder`. `proscenium_core.scala` adds the Predef members worth
    keeping (`nn`, `summon`, `identity`, `locally`, `valueOf`, `is`, `ArrowAssoc`, the
    primitive wrappers and array ops, `assert`, `???`), `ClassTag`, `Typeable`, `TreeMap`,
    `TreeSet`, `TrieMap`, `NonFatal`, `boundary`/`break`, the `…HasAsScala`/`…HasAsJava`
    converters, `tailrec`/`targetName`/`switch`/`missingContext`, the `unchecked…`
    annotations, and a few of its own: `classOf` (an inline version which is correct through
    an export), `absolve` (Predef's `runtimeChecked`), `Void`, `Nat`, `Label`, `Zero`,
    `Mono`, `~>`, `prohibits`, `infer`, `provide`, `->` and the `unexported` annotation.

    What is *not* blessed is as deliberate as what is. The stdlib's concrete collections
    (`Seq`, `Vector`, `IndexedSeq`, `Range`, `LazyList`) are absent, so a stdlib collection
    at an interop seam must announce itself with `import scala.collection…`; `Ordering` and
    `Ordered` are absent, since comparison is `Comparable` and the few APIs which still take
    an `Ordering` import `scala.math.Ordering` explicitly; `Iterator` is absent, so a bare
    `Iterator` under `import soundness.*` is murmuration's separation-checked one, and a
    stdlib iterator is written `scala.collection.Iterator`; and `Precise` is absent because
    an exported alias of it silently fails to suppress union widening — write
    `scala.Precise`. Packages (`scala.caps`, `scala.math`, `scala.compiletime`,
    `scala.annotation`, `scala.util`) cannot be exported and are imported per file.

    Two traps for macro authors. `Unit` is a type alias here, not an export (its companion
    cannot be exported), so it is a *distinct symbol* from `scala.Unit`: in a macro, compare
    types with `=:=` or after `.dealias`, never by `typeSymbol ==`. And `Array` is the
    opaque alias, so `TypeRepr.of[Array[Byte]]` is likewise not `scala.Array`'s symbol.

    The library is a single source set, `proscenium.core`, with no dependencies, compiled
    for the JVM, Scala.js and Scala Native. On every platform the collections are the same
    stdlib structures underneath; `Array` is the platform's native array.

refer proscenium.List
refer proscenium.Array
refer proscenium.Populated
refer proscenium.Comparison
refer proscenium.Chain

entry .Array   a fixed-size array whose write access is tracked by separation checking
  detail
      `Array[element]` is an opaque alias of the platform's native array, so it costs
      nothing to use, but its access rights are in its capture set: any reference can
      *read*, only an exclusive reference — `Array[element]^` — can *write*, aliased
      writers are rejected, and `freeze` consumes an exclusive array to yield the
      **frozen** form, `Array[element]^{}`, without copying. The frozen form is not a
      parallel immutable type: an array no capture set can write through *is* immutable,
      so `^{}` is the whole story, and `Data`, anticipation's type for bytes, is exactly
      `Array[Byte]^{}`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val frozen: Array[Int]^{} = Array(1, 2, 3)        // a literal is frozen by construction
      val buffer: Array[Int]^ = Array.allocate[Int](3)  // exclusive and writable
      buffer(0) = 11
      buffer.fill(8)
      val done: Array[Int]^{} = Array.freeze(buffer)    // consumes `buffer`
      Array.freeze(Array.thaw(done)).to[List]            // an exclusive copy, frozen again
      done.map(_*2).to[List]                             // List(16, 16, 16)
      ```

      Reading is `Int`-indexed here, since `denominative`'s `Ordinal` sits above this
      module: an exclusive array reads with `apply(index)` and writes with
      `update(index, value)`, both inlined to the JVM's own array access; a shared or frozen
      reference reads with `readUnchecked(index)`, which is bounds-partial and is what
      concordance's total, `Optional`-returning `at` and the ordinal-indexed `apply` in
      rudiments are built on. At an ordinary call site use those, or a confined ordinal;
      a bare `readUnchecked` carries a comment naming its proof.

      Editing a frozen array is copy-and-freeze: `thaw` for an exclusive copy to write
      several slots, `concat` of segments (`Array.concat(xs.before(i), replacement,
      xs.after(i))`) for a splice, and rudiments' `define` for one slot. Two things are
      sanctioned uses of the raw `scala.Array`: `raw` and `unsafeJvm`, for the JDK's
      byte-array surface (`OutputStream.write`, `MessageDigest.update`), and a field whose
      type an external definition fixes. New code otherwise stays on `Array`.

      Equality on an array is reference identity — the opaque alias cannot override `==` —
      so content equality is `Array.same`, and a test compares through `to[List]`. A `val`
      cannot hold an exclusive buffer that is then consumed and replaced (`grow`), so an
      accumulating builder threads its buffer through `consume` parameters by recursion.
      One trap: `import rudiments.*` brings an `Applicable` `apply` which shadows the
      exclusive array's inline `apply`; a file which imports rudiments wholesale reads a
      buffer through ordinals or `readUnchecked` instead.

      Pattern matching reads but never writes, so `case Array(a, b, c)` works on any
      reference. A frozen array `map`s to a frozen array (`map` needs a `ClassTag` for the
      result element, which is why it is a companion extension rather than a `Mappable`
      instance), and the generic collection vocabulary — `to[List]`, `each`, `fold`,
      `extent`, `at` — comes from the libraries above.
  refer proscenium.Sized
  refer proscenium.Populated
  entry .allocate   a new exclusive array of a given size: `Array.allocate[Int](3)`
      Named `allocate` rather than `apply` so that `Array(3)` unambiguously means the
      one-element literal. The elements are the type's zero (`0`, `false`, `null`), and
      the result is `Array[element]^`, writable through `update`. Needs a `ClassTag`.
  entry .apply   `Array(a, b, c)`, a frozen literal; and `buffer(index)`, the exclusive read
      Two members share the name. On the companion, `Array(elements*)` builds a fresh
      array which no writer can alias, so it is frozen (`Array[element]^{}`) by
      construction; it needs a `ClassTag`. As an extension on an exclusive `Array[element]^`,
      `buffer(index)` reads one slot, inlined to the JVM's own array access (no boxing):
      sole ownership means nobody else can have changed the content. On a shared or frozen
      reference there is no `apply`; read with `readUnchecked`, or through concordance's
      total `at`.
  entry .update   `buffer(index) = value`: writes one slot of an exclusive array
      An inline extension on `Array[element]^` only — a frozen or shared reference has no
      `update` — compiled to the JVM's own array store. The receiver is inline, so it is
      evaluated after the index and value, where it is used.
  entry .empty   the frozen empty array: `Array.empty[Int]`
  entry .from   a frozen array copied from any `IterableOnce`
      `Array.from(elements)` copies the elements of a stdlib iterable or iterator into a
      fresh array, which is frozen since nothing else can reach it. Needs a `ClassTag`.
  entry .fill   `Array.fill(n)(element)`: a frozen array of `n` copies; and `buffer.fill(value)`
      On the companion, `Array.fill(count)(element)` evaluates `element` once per slot and
      freezes the result; when `count` is a literal the result is additionally branded
      `Sized[count]`, so `Array.fill(512)(0.toByte)` is an `Array[Byte]^{} & Sized[512]`
      and a literal ordinal below 512 reads it totally. A `val` count gives the plain
      array. As an extension on an exclusive `Array[element]^`, `buffer.fill(value)`
      overwrites every slot in place.
  entry .tabulate   `Array.tabulate(n)(index => …)`: a frozen array computed by index
      Like `.fill` with the slot's index passed to the lambda, and branded `Sized[n]` when
      `n` is a literal.
  entry .range   `Array.range(start, end)`: the integers from `start` up to `end`, exclusive
      Frozen; an empty range (`end <= start`) is the empty array.
  entry .concat   a frozen array of two or three readable arrays in sequence
      `Array.concat(left, right)` copies both into a fresh array and freezes it; with two
      `Sized` arguments the result is `Sized[left + right]`, decided at the type level. The
      three-operand form, `Array.concat(left, middle, right)`, is the splice: `Array.concat
      (xs.before(i), replacement, xs.after(i))` rebuilds an array with a segment replaced
      in one pass. The arity is fixed rather than variadic because a frozen array as the
      element of a varargs `Seq` is re-freshened under capture checking and the segments'
      element types then fail to unify. Needs a `ClassTag`.
  entry .same   element-wise equality of two readable arrays
      `Array.same(left, right)` is whether the two have the same length and equal elements
      at every index. `==` on arrays is reference identity, which the opaque alias cannot
      override, so content equality is a named operation.
  entry .freeze   consumes an exclusive array and yields it frozen, without copying
      `Array.freeze(buffer)` takes `buffer` as a `consume` parameter: the separation
      checker retires every writer statically, so the surviving reference can drop its
      write capability and become `Array[element]^{}` with no copy. It is the only sound
      way to produce a frozen array from a mutable one; after the call, `buffer` cannot be
      used again.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val buffer = Array.allocate[Int](2)
      buffer(0) = 1
      buffer(1) = 2
      Array.freeze(buffer).to[List]   // List(1, 2)
      ```
  entry .thaw   an exclusive copy of any readable array, the inverse of `freeze`
      `Array.thaw(array)` allocates a fresh exclusive array and copies the source into it,
      so the caller may write several slots (`copy(i) = value`) and `freeze` again. Always
      a copy, never the source itself: the source may be frozen or shared, so other
      readers keep seeing it unchanged. For a one-slot edit use rudiments' `define`; for a
      splice, `.concat`. Needs a `ClassTag`.
  entry .grow   a larger exclusive array holding an exclusive buffer's content, consuming it
      `Array.grow(buffer, size)` is the step of a linear accumulating builder: it consumes
      `buffer`, copies as much of it as fits into a fresh array of `size` (a smaller size
      truncates), and returns that, exclusive. Because the old buffer is consumed, the
      idiom is recursion threading the buffer through `consume` parameters — a `var`
      cannot hold an exclusive buffer.
  entry .place   copies a readable array into an exclusive one, at an offset
      Extensions on `Array[element]^`: `buffer.place(source)` copies `source` to the start,
      `buffer.place(source, at)` to offset `at`, and `buffer.place(source, sourceStart,
      targetStart, count)` copies a window. Frozen and shared sources both serve. It is
      `System.arraycopy` underneath, and is what every frozen constructor uses to fill its
      result.
  entry .length   the number of slots, on any reference
  entry .readUnchecked   reads a slot of a shared or frozen array, with no bounds check
      The bounds-partial read, inlined: `array.readUnchecked(index)` on any readable
      reference. Reading through a shared reference is sound because separation excludes
      a live writer wherever readers alias. It is the primitive under concordance's total
      `at`; at an ordinary call site prefer that or a confined ordinal, and a bare call
      carries a comment naming the proof that the index is in range.
  entry .raw   the underlying JVM array of an exclusive reference, for interop
      `buffer.raw` is the `scala.Array[element]^` behind an exclusive array, for a Java API
      which fills it (`random.nextBytes`, `stream.read`). The result aliases the array and
      shares its exclusivity rather than escaping it.
  entry .stdlib   a zero-copy, read-only `ArraySeq` view of any readable array
      The greppable bridge to the stdlib and Java APIs which want a `Seq`, as
      `List.stdlib` is for lists. Not for element access: its generic `apply` boxes and
      is a partial read the collection vocabulary does not offer.
  entry .map   a frozen array of the results of a function, from any readable array
      `array.map(lambda)` on any readable reference gives a new frozen array; it needs a
      `ClassTag` for the result element, which is why it is a companion extension rather
      than a `Mappable` instance (no typeclass slot can carry the obligation). The lambda's
      result type is an explicit type parameter so that a nested frozen array's capture is
      not re-minted at the call.
  entry .scratch   a bare JVM array, for interior scratch below the exclusivity discipline
      `Array.scratch[element](size)` is `new scala.Array[element](size)`, through the
      companion so that the fields of `caps.Mutable` state machines and other code beneath
      `Array`'s reach share this vocabulary without spelling `new`. Not an `Array`; see
      `.raw` for the exclusive interop view.
  entry .unsafeFrozen   asserts a JVM array has no writer and returns it frozen
      The trusted boundary cast for interop edges which cannot thread `consume`: the
      caller asserts it retains no writer to the `scala.Array` it passes. Named `unsafe…`
      because the assertion is unchecked.
  entry .unsafeJvm   the JVM array behind a readable reference, for Java APIs which only read
      `Array.unsafeJvm(array)` hands a `scala.Array[element]` to the JDK's byte-array
      surface — `OutputStream.write`, `MessageDigest.update`, `SecretKeySpec` — which reads
      but never writes its argument. Nothing is asserted about this side; the whole claim
      is about the callee, which a Java signature cannot express, so it is one greppable
      call checkable against one Javadoc rather than an ambient `Unsafe`.
  entry .unapplySeq   `case Array(a, b, c)`: pattern support for any readable array
      A pattern reads but never writes, so frozen, shared and exclusive receivers all
      match; a pattern of the wrong arity fails to match.
  entry .quoted   lifts a list or frozen array of `Expr`s into one `Expr` of a frozen array
      The native counterpart of `Expr.ofList` for macro code holding `List[Expr[element]]`
      or `Array[Expr[element]]^{}`: `Array.quoted(elements)` splices one
      `Expr[Array[element]^{}]`, resolving the element's `ClassTag` at the expansion site
      where the type is concrete, and failing the expansion with _no ClassTag is available
      for the elements of the array_ if there is none.
  entry .arrayIsSpreadable   lets a frozen array be spliced into varargs: `f(array*)`
      The `Spreadable` given (a `scala.Spreadable`, from the proscala compiler) which
      makes `f(array*)` work on an `Array[element]^{}` without a `stdlib` crossing.

entry .List   an immutable linked list, as an opaque alias of the stdlib's `List`
  detail
      `List[element]` is the workhorse collection: cheap to prepend to and to traverse,
      linear to index or to count. It is an opaque alias of
      `scala.collection.immutable.List`, so it costs nothing, but none of the stdlib's
      members are visible on it; its API is the typeclass-driven vocabulary of the
      libraries above (`map`, `filter`, `bind`, `fold`, `each`, `head`, `at`, `sort`, …),
      which is total. The companion holds construction, pattern support, the `stdlib`
      bridge, and the macro lifting.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val numbers = List(3, 1, 2)      // List[Int] & Populated
      numbers.head                      // 3: total, since the arity proved non-emptiness
      val more = 0 :: numbers           // List(0, 3, 1, 2)

      more match
        case head :: tail => head
        case Nil          => -1

      List.empty[Int].head              // Optional[Int]: Unset
      ```

      A literal of one to twelve elements is branded `& Populated` — the arity of the
      call *is* the proof of non-emptiness — so `head`, `last`, `tail` and `reduce` are
      total on it. A thirteen-element literal, `List(elements*)` from a `Seq`, and the
      result of any transformation are plain `List[element]`, on which `head` is an
      `Optional`; rudiments' `occupied` recovers the proof with one check. A branded list
      compares with a plain one (`.populatedEquality`).

      `Nil` is the empty list, matched by equality (`case Nil =>`); `head :: tail`
      constructs and deconstructs (through `consConstructor` and the `::` extractor); and
      `case List(a, b)` matches by `.unapplySeq`. Counting a list (`size`) and indexing it
      (`at`, `sec`, `ter`) are walks from its head, and so are available only under
      `import dysasymptotics.linearSize` and `linearAccess` respectively — which say
      something true and should not be dodged through `stdlib`.

      Crossing to the stdlib is `list.stdlib`, a cast with no copy, and back is `List.from`
      or `.to(List)` on any stdlib collection (through `.factory`); there is no implicit
      conversion in either direction. A varargs splice `f(list*)` works directly. The
      companion's primitive operations (`.size`, `.head`, `.at`, `.map`, …) are plain
      methods for the typeclass instances above to call; at an ordinary call site the
      extension vocabulary is what to use.
  refer proscenium.Nil
  refer proscenium.Populated
  refer proscenium.Sequence
  refer proscenium.Chain
  entry .apply   `List(a, b, c)`: a list of its arguments, branded `Populated` up to twelve
      One fixed-arity overload per arity from one to twelve returns `List[element] &
      Populated`; the varargs form, which a `Seq` splice or more than twelve elements
      reaches, returns a plain `List[element]`. The fixed arities exist because a
      `(head, tail*)` overload is ambiguous with `(elements*)` and a macro would be an
      implicit search under live type variables.
  entry .empty   the empty list, typed: `List.empty[Int]`
  entry .from   a list copied from any `IterableOnce`
      `List.from(elements)` is the way back from the stdlib: it copies a stdlib collection
      or iterator into a `List`. A `scala.collection.immutable.List` is wrapped without
      copying.
  entry .fill   `List.fill(n)(element)`: `n` evaluations of `element`
  entry .tabulate   `List.tabulate(n)(index => …)`: `n` elements computed by index
  entry .range   `List.range(start, end)`: the integers from `start` up to `end`, exclusive
  entry .unapplySeq   `case List(a, b)`: pattern support
  entry .stdlib   the underlying `scala.collection.immutable.List`, with no copy
      The greppable bridge to a stdlib or Java API, as an inline cast. It is the one way to
      reach the partial stdlib surface, and every use is a visible seam; it should not be
      used to bypass a cost gate (`xs.stdlib.length` for a gated `size`).
  entry .factory   `.to(List)` on a stdlib collection builds a `List`
      A `Conversion` from the companion `List.type` to a stdlib `Factory`, so that
      `scalaSeq.to(List)` works. It is defined on the companion's type only, so it cannot
      expose members of `List` values.
  entry .populatedEquality   `CanEqual[List[a] & Populated, List[b]]`: a branded list compares with a plain one
      `CanEqual` is sought in the companions of the compared types, and the `Populated`
      brand contributes none, so the equality lives here; `.populatedEqualityReversed`
      is the other order.
  entry .populatedEqualityReversed   `CanEqual[List[a], List[b] & Populated]`
  entry .toExpr   lifts a `List` of liftable elements into an `Expr` in a macro
      `Expr(list)` works for a `List[element]` whose element has a `ToExpr`; the lifted tree
      is `List.from(…)`.
  entry .fromExpr   unlifts an `Expr[List[element]]` which was produced by lifting
      Recognises `List.from(…)` of a constant stdlib list, `List.empty` and `Nil`; any
      other list-yielding tree is not constant-foldable and gives `None`.
  entry .quoted   lifts a `List[Expr[element]]` into one `Expr[List[element]]`
      The native counterpart of `Expr.ofList`, for macro code holding a native list of
      lifted elements; `Expr.ofList(xs.stdlib)` wrapped in `List.from` is `List.quoted(xs)`.
  entry .size   the length, by traversal: a primitive for `Countable`
      The companion's primitive operations are plain methods, not extensions, for the
      typeclass instances in the libraries above; call-site code uses the gated `size`
      extension instead.
  entry .nil   whether the list is empty: a primitive for the typeclass instances
  entry .head   the first element, partial: a primitive for the `Populated`-gated `head`
  entry .tail   everything after the first element, partial: a primitive
  entry .last   the final element, partial: a primitive
  entry .lead   everything before the last element, partial: a primitive
  entry .at   the element at an `Int` index, partial: a primitive for `Indexable`
  entry .define   a list with the element at an index replaced: a primitive for `Definable`
  entry .has   whether an element is present: a primitive for `Inclusive`
  entry .drop   the list without its first `n` elements: a primitive
  entry .append   the list with an element added at the end, branded `Populated`
      Appending makes the list non-empty, so the construction is the proof.
  entry .prepend   the list with an element added at the front, branded `Populated`
  entry .concat   two lists joined: a primitive for `concat`
  entry .invert   the list reversed: a primitive for `Reversible`
  entry .map   the list with a function applied to each element: a primitive for `Mappable`
  entry .slice   the elements between two `Int` indices: a primitive for `excerpt`
  entry .iterator   a stdlib iterator over the list: a primitive for `Traversable`

entry .Nil   the empty list, `List[Nothing]`
    A value, not an extractor object, so `case Nil =>` matches by equality and existing
    syntax compiles unchanged. It conforms to every `List[element]` by covariance, and
    `Default[List[element]]` is `Nil`.

entry .listIsSpreadable   lets a list be spliced into varargs: `f(list*)`
    The `Spreadable` given (a `scala.Spreadable`, from the proscala compiler) which makes
    `f(list*)` work on an opaque `List` without a `stdlib` crossing. It is
    subtype-parametric, so a branded `List[a] & Populated` splices too. Unlike the other
    collections' `Spreadable` givens it sits at the top level of the package rather than
    in the companion, which is equivalent for resolution: a top-level opaque type's
    implicit scope includes the definitions beside it.

entry .consConstructor   the given which supplies `head :: tail` on the opaque `List`
    `::` is right-associative, so the receiver of the extension is the head — the left
    operand at the call site — and the argument is the tail. The extension rides on a given
    rather than at top level because the name belongs to the extractor object `::`; the
    given is visible everywhere through `-Yimports`, so `1 :: List(2, 3)` just works. The
    result is a plain `List[element]`, not `& Populated`: in a chain `a :: b :: c` the
    right operand is typed first, and the element type cannot be inferred from a branded
    result there.

entry .::   the extractor for `case head :: tail =>` on the opaque `List`
  detail
      A name-based extractor, allocating no `Option`: `::.unapply(list)` yields a
      `ConsView` whose `isEmpty` is the list's, and whose `_1` and `_2` are the head and
      the tail. The constructor `head :: tail` is the extension in `consConstructor`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      List(1, 2, 3) match
        case head :: tail => head
        case Nil          => 0
      ```
  entry .ConsView   the view a `::` pattern reads: `isEmpty`, `_1` (head), `_2` (tail)
    entry #isEmpty   whether the list is empty, in which case the pattern fails
    entry #get   the view itself
    entry #_1   the head
    entry #_2   the tail, as a `List`
  entry .unapply   `case head :: tail`: the view over a list

entry .Sequence   an immutable indexed sequence, as an opaque alias of the stdlib's `Vector`
  detail
      A `Sequence[element]` is cheap to read at any position and to append to, so
      indexing (`at`, `sec`, `ter`) and `size` need no cost acknowledgment, unlike a
      `List`. It is an opaque alias of `scala.collection.immutable.Vector` with the same
      design as `List`: members invisible, the vocabulary from the typeclass extensions
      above, construction and the `stdlib` bridge in the companion, and no implicit
      conversion to or from `Vector`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val letters = Sequence(t"a", t"b", t"c")   // Sequence[Text] & Populated
      letters(Sec)                                // t"b"
      letters.head                                // t"a"

      letters match
        case Sequence(a, b, c) => a
        case _                 => t""
      ```

      A literal of one to twelve elements is branded `& Populated`, as a `List` literal is.
      A `Sequence` has no `factory` conversion, so a stdlib collection is converted with
      `Sequence.from(elements)` rather than `.to(Sequence)`.
  refer proscenium.List
  entry .apply   `Sequence(a, b, c)`: a sequence of its arguments, branded `Populated` up to twelve
  entry .empty   the empty sequence, typed: `Sequence.empty[Int]`
  entry .from   a sequence copied from any `IterableOnce`
      The way back from the stdlib, and the only one: `Sequence` has no `factory`
      conversion for `.to(Sequence)`.
  entry .unapplySeq   `case Sequence(a, b)`: pattern support
  entry .stdlib   the underlying `scala.collection.immutable.Vector`, with no copy
      The greppable bridge to a stdlib or Java API.
  entry .populatedEquality   `CanEqual[Sequence[a] & Populated, Sequence[b]]`
