  entry .map   the sequence with a function applied to each element: a primitive
  entry .slice   the elements between two `Int` indices: a primitive
  entry .iterator   a stdlib iterator over the sequence: a primitive for `Traversable`

entry .Set   an immutable set, as an opaque alias of the stdlib's `Set`
  detail
      A `Set[element]` holds each element once, with no order, so it cannot be sorted,
      excerpted or indexed: those operations are absent rather than arbitrary. It supports
      membership (`has`), insertion (`:+`), union (`concat`), `intersect` and `except`
      through the typeclass extensions above, and is invariant in its element, like the
      stdlib's. An opaque alias of `scala.collection.immutable.Set` with the same design as
      `List`: members invisible, construction and the `stdlib` bridge in the companion, no
      implicit conversion.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val primes = Set(2, 3, 5, 7)
      primes.has(5)                  // true
      primes.intersect(Set(2, 4))    // Set(2)
      primes.except(Set(2, 3))       // Set(5, 7)
      primes.stdlib                  // scala.collection.immutable.Set(2, 3, 5, 7)
      ```

      Unlike `List` and `Sequence`, a `Set` literal is not branded `Populated`; `occupied`
      proves it where needed.
  refer proscenium.Map
  entry .apply   `Set(a, b, c)`: a set of its arguments, duplicates discarded
  entry .empty   the empty set, typed: `Set.empty[Int]`
  entry .from   a set built from any `IterableOnce`, duplicates discarded
  entry .stdlib   the underlying `scala.collection.immutable.Set`, with no copy
  entry .factory   `.to(Set)` on a stdlib collection builds a `Set`
  entry .populatedEquality   `CanEqual[Set[a] & Populated, Set[b]]`: a proven-non-empty set compares with a plain one
  entry .populatedEqualityReversed   `CanEqual[Set[a], Set[b] & Populated]`
  entry .toExpr   lifts a `Set` of liftable elements into an `Expr` in a macro
  entry .fromExpr   unlifts an `Expr[Set[element]]` produced by `Set.from` of a constant list
  entry .size   the number of elements, constant time: a primitive for `Countable`
      The companion's primitive operations are plain methods for the typeclass instances
      above; call-site code uses the extension vocabulary.
  entry .nil   whether the set is empty: a primitive
  entry .has   whether an element is present: a primitive for `Inclusive`
  entry .insert   the set with an element added: a primitive for `:+`
  entry .concat   the union of two sets: a primitive
  entry .intersect   the elements in both sets: a primitive
  entry .except   the elements of the left set not in the right: a primitive
  entry .map   the set of a function's results over the elements: a primitive for `Mappable`
  entry .iterator   a stdlib iterator over the elements: a primitive for `Traversable`

entry .Map   an immutable map from keys to values, as an opaque alias of the stdlib's `Map`
  detail
      A `Map[key, value]` pairs keys with values with no promised iteration order (a
      `Ledger` promises insertion order). Through the typeclass extensions above it reads a
      value by key with `at`, as an `Optional` since the key may be absent; tests a key with
      `defines`; adds or replaces with `define`; removes with `omit`; exposes `keys` (a
      `Set`) and `values`; and `map`s its values, keeping its keys, where `remap`
      transforms entries as pairs. An opaque alias of `scala.collection.immutable.Map`,
      covariant in its value, with construction and the `stdlib` bridge in the companion
      and no implicit conversion.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val ages = Map(t"Ada" -> 36, t"Bob" -> 41)
      ages.at(t"Ada")          // 36
      ages.at(t"Cy")           // Unset
      ages.define(t"Cy", 29)   // a new map of three entries; `ages` is unchanged
      ages.map(_ + 1)          // Map(t"Ada" -> 37, t"Bob" -> 42)
      ```
  refer proscenium.Ledger
  refer proscenium.Set
  entry .apply   `Map(k1 -> v1, k2 -> v2)`: a map of its pairs; a later pair for a key wins
  entry .empty   the empty map, typed: `Map.empty[Text, Int]`
  entry .from   a map built from any `IterableOnce` of pairs
  entry .stdlib   the underlying `scala.collection.immutable.Map`, with no copy
  entry .factory   `.to(Map)` on a stdlib collection of pairs builds a `Map`
  entry .toExpr   lifts a `Map` of liftable keys and values into an `Expr` in a macro
  entry .fromExpr   unlifts an `Expr[Map[key, value]]` produced by `Map.from` of a constant list
  entry .size   the number of entries, constant time: a primitive for `Countable`
      The companion's primitive operations are plain methods for the typeclass instances
      above; call-site code uses the extension vocabulary. `.read` mirrors the stdlib's
      `Option`-returning `get`; the `Optional`-returning `at` is layered above, in the
      libraries which know `vacuous`.
  entry .nil   whether the map is empty: a primitive
  entry .defines   whether a key is present: a primitive for `Applicable`
  entry .at   the value for a key, partial: a primitive
  entry .read   the value for a key as an `Option`: a primitive, mirroring the stdlib's `get`
  entry .define   a map with a key set to a value: a primitive for `Definable`
  entry .omit   a map without a key: a primitive for `Omissible`
  entry .keys   the keys, as a `Set`: a primitive
  entry .values   the values, as a `List`: a primitive
  entry .concat   two maps merged, the right's entries winning: a primitive
  entry .map   the map with a function applied to each value, keys unchanged: a primitive for `Mappable`
  entry .iterator   a stdlib iterator over the entries as pairs: a primitive for `Traversable`

entry .Ledger   an insertion-ordered immutable map, as an opaque alias of `VectorMap`
  detail
      A `Ledger[key, value]` is exactly a `Map` which promises to iterate its entries in
      the order they were first added, which `Map` deliberately does not. It is backed by
      `scala.collection.immutable.VectorMap` — insertion-ordered like `ListMap`, but with
      effectively constant-time lookup and append, since the registries kept in ledgers
      (dialect member tables, attribute lists, test reports) grow into the hundreds. The
      vocabulary is the `Map`'s: `at`, `defines`, `define`, `omit`, `keys`, `values`,
      `map` over values preserving order. A `Ledger` is not a `Map` and does not convert to
      one.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val ledger = Ledger(t"c" -> 3, t"a" -> 1, t"b" -> 2)
      ledger.keys                    // List(t"c", t"a", t"b"), in insertion order
      ledger.at(t"a")                // 1
      Ledger(t"a" -> 1, t"a" -> 2).at(t"a")   // 2: a later pair for the same key wins
      ```
  refer proscenium.Map
  entry .apply   `Ledger(k1 -> v1, k2 -> v2)`: a ledger of its pairs, in order
  entry .empty   the empty ledger, typed: `Ledger.empty[Text, Int]`
  entry .from   a ledger built from any `IterableOnce` of pairs, in their order
  entry .stdlib   the underlying `scala.collection.immutable.VectorMap`, with no copy
  entry .factory   `.to(Ledger)` on a stdlib collection of pairs builds a `Ledger`
  entry .toExpr   lifts a `Ledger` of liftable keys and values into an `Expr` in a macro
  entry .fromExpr   unlifts an `Expr[Ledger[key, value]]` produced by `Ledger.from` of a constant list
  entry .size   the number of entries: a primitive for `Countable`
      The companion's primitive operations are plain methods for the typeclass instances
      above; call-site code uses the extension vocabulary.
  entry .nil   whether the ledger is empty: a primitive
  entry .defines   whether a key is present: a primitive
  entry .at   the value for a key, partial: a primitive
  entry .read   the value for a key as an `Option`: a primitive, mirroring the stdlib's `get`
  entry .define   a ledger with a key set to a value, keeping its position if present: a primitive
  entry .omit   a ledger without a key: a primitive
  entry .keys   the keys as a `List`, in insertion order: a primitive
  entry .values   the values as a `List`, in insertion order: a primitive
  entry .concat   two ledgers merged, the right's entries appended or overriding: a primitive
  entry .map   the ledger with a function applied to each value, order preserved: a primitive
  entry .iterator   a stdlib iterator over the entries, in insertion order: a primitive

entry .Chain   a lazy, possibly infinite sequence, as an opaque alias of the stdlib's `LazyList`
  detail
      A `Chain[element]` computes its elements as they are demanded and remembers them,
      so it can describe every natural number or every line a process will print, as long
      as only a finite prefix is ever forced. `head #:: rest` prepends without forcing
      `rest`; `Chain.iterate`, `Chain.unfold` and `Chain.continually` generate from a seed
      or a value; `keep` and `skip` take and drop a prefix lazily; `prefix #::: suffix`
      concatenates without forcing the suffix; and `case head #:: tail =>` matches forcing
      only the head. `size`, `last` and `lead` force the whole chain and may never return,
      so they are gated behind `import dysasymptotics.unboundedSize`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val naturals: Chain[Int] = Chain.iterate(0)(_ + 1)
      naturals.keep(5).to[List]            // List(0, 1, 2, 3, 4)
      naturals.skip(10).keep(2).to[List]   // List(10, 11)
      Chain.continually(7).keep(3).to[List]

      def evens: Chain[Int] = naturals.filter(_%2 == 0)
      evens match
        case head #:: _ => head             // 0, forcing nothing beyond it
      ```

      An opaque alias of `scala.collection.immutable.LazyList` with the same design as
      `List` — members invisible, `stdlib` bridge in the companion, no implicit conversion
      — with one difference: a `Chain` literal is never branded `Populated`, since the
      streaming shape must not let a transparent expansion into a capture-checked call
      site; prove non-emptiness with `occupied` or a `#::` match. There is a `ToExpr` but
      no `FromExpr`, since an eager unlift would misrepresent the lazy shape. A `Chain`
      has deliberately no `Segmentable` instance: the lazy `keep`/`skip` in
      `chainSegments` are what it has, and a strict instance would force an unbounded
      chain.
  refer proscenium.lazyCons
  refer proscenium.chainSegments
  refer proscenium.List
  entry .apply   `Chain(a, b, c)`: a finite chain of its arguments, unbranded
  entry .empty   the empty chain, typed: `Chain.empty[Int]`
  entry .from   a chain over any `IterableOnce`, evaluated lazily where the source allows
  entry .continually   an infinite chain repeating a by-name value: `Chain.continually(7)`
  entry .iterate   an infinite chain from a seed and a step: `Chain.iterate(0)(_ + 1)`
      The seed is by-name; nothing is evaluated until forced.
  entry .unfold   a chain generated from a state by a function yielding `Option[(element, state)]`
      `Chain.unfold(init)(state => …)` continues while the function returns `Some` of the
      next element and state, and ends at `None`.
  entry .range   `Chain.range(start, end)`: the integral values from `start` up to `end`
  entry .cons   the primitive lazy cons: `Chain.cons(head, tail)`, with `tail` by-name
      The method form of `#::`.
  entry .defer   a chain whose evaluation is deferred until it is forced
      `Chain.defer(chain)` keeps the by-name `chain` unevaluated until the result is
      demanded, for a recursive definition whose body must not run eagerly.
  entry .unapplySeq   `case Chain(a, b)`: pattern support, forcing the whole chain
  entry .stdlib   the underlying `scala.collection.immutable.LazyList`, with no copy
  entry .factory   `.to(Chain)` on a stdlib collection builds a `Chain`
  entry .populatedEquality   `CanEqual[Chain[a] & Populated, Chain[b]]`
  entry .populatedEqualityReversed   `CanEqual[Chain[a], Chain[b] & Populated]`
  entry .toExpr   lifts a finite `Chain` into an `Expr` in a macro, forcing it entirely
      A constant tree cannot be lazy, so lifting forces the whole chain; it serves only a
      finite chain a macro has already materialised. There is deliberately no `FromExpr`.
  entry .chainIsSpreadable   lets a chain be spliced into varargs: `f(chain*)`, forcing it
  entry .size   the length, forcing the whole chain: a primitive for the `unboundedSize`-gated `size`
      The companion's primitive operations are plain methods for the typeclass instances
      above; `size`, `last` and `lead` force the whole chain and diverge on an infinite
      one, which is why their extensions are gated.
  entry .nil   whether the chain is empty, forcing only the first node: a primitive
  entry .head   the first element, partial: a primitive for the `Populated`-gated `head`
  entry .tail   the chain after its first element, not forcing the tail's contents: a primitive
  entry .last   the final element, forcing the whole chain: a primitive
  entry .lead   everything before the last element, forcing the whole chain: a primitive
  entry .append   the chain with an element lazily appended: a primitive
  entry .prepend   the chain with an element prepended, lazily: a primitive
  entry .concat   two chains joined lazily: a primitive
  entry .map   the chain with a function applied lazily to each element: a primitive
  entry .iterator   a stdlib iterator over the chain: a primitive for `Traversable`

entry .lazyCons   the given which supplies `head #:: tail` on the opaque `Chain`
    The lazy cons constructor: `head #:: tail` prepends `head` to `tail` without
    evaluating `tail`, which is by-name, so `head #:: recur()` does not call `recur()`.
    As with `::`, the receiver of the extension is the head (right-associativity swaps the
    operands), and it rides on a given because the name belongs to the extractor object
    `#::`; the given is visible everywhere through `-Yimports`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    def from(n: Int): Chain[Int] = n #:: from(n + 1)
    from(1).keep(3).to[List]   // List(1, 2, 3)
    ```

entry .#::   the non-forcing extractor for `case head #:: tail =>` on a `Chain`
  detail
      A name-based extractor whose `isEmpty` and `_1` force only the first node, and
      whose `_2` is the tail through `LazyList#tail`, which does not force the tail's
      elements — so matching a chain to read its head computes exactly one element. The
      constructor `head #:: tail` is the extension in `lazyCons`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Chain.continually(42) match
        case head #:: _ => head   // 42
      ```
  entry .ConsView   the view a `#::` pattern reads: `isEmpty`, `_1` (head), `_2` (lazy tail)
    entry #isEmpty   whether the chain is empty, forcing only the first node
    entry #get   the view itself
    entry #_1   the head
    entry #_2   the tail, as a `Chain`, unforced
  entry .unapply   `case head #:: tail`: the view over a chain

entry .#:::   `prefix #::: suffix`: lazy concatenation of two chains
    An extension on `Chain[element]`: the suffix is by-name and is not forced until the
    prefix is exhausted, so a chain may be defined as a finite prefix followed by a
    recursive or infinite suffix. The suffix's element type may widen the prefix's.

entry .chainSegments   the given which supplies the lazy `keep` and `skip` on a `Chain`
    `chain.keep(n)` and `chain.skip(n)` take and drop a prefix by count, `chain.keep(p)`
    and `chain.skip(p)` by predicate; all four are non-forcing — they evaluate elements
    only as the result is demanded — so all are safe on an unbounded chain, where the
    `Segmentable`-driven `keep`/`skip` on the strict collections would count and rebuild.
    The count forms are total: an out-of-range count clips. There is no right-to-left
    form, since it would have no non-forcing meaning. They ride on a given so that they
    are consulted only after the generic candidates have failed on a `Chain` receiver,
    which they always do.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    Chain.iterate(0)(_ + 1).skip(10).keep(2).to[List]   // List(10, 11)
    ```

entry .Comparison   the result of a three-way comparison: `Less`, `Same` or `More`
  detail
      The currency of symbolism's `Comparable`, whose `compare(left, right)` returns one.
      An enumeration rather than an `Int` sign: the three cases are singletons, so it
      allocates nothing, while remaining pattern-matchable and carrying its queries as
      members, which an extension named `also` or `more` could not do without contending
      with the universal extensions in the umbrella. `Less` means the left operand is
      smaller, `More` that it is larger, and `Same` that neither is.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Comparison(-42)                          // Comparison.Less
      Comparison.Less.flip                     // Comparison.More
      Comparison.More.sign                     // 1
      Comparison.Same.also(Comparison.Less)    // Comparison.Less
      Comparison.More.also(Comparison.Less)    // Comparison.More
      ```

      `also` composes comparisons lexicographically — this one unless it is `Same`, in
      which case the next, which is by-name and so unevaluated when the first is decisive —
      and `sign` and `.apply` convert to and from the sign convention of
      `java.util.Comparator` and `scala.math.Ordering` at the seams.
  entry .apply   a `Comparison` from a `compareTo`-style sign: negative, zero or positive
      Magnitudes are collapsed to the three cases, so every `Comparison` is canonical.
  entry #less   whether this is `Less`
  entry #same   whether this is `Same`
  entry #more   whether this is `More`
  entry #flip   the comparison of the same two values in the opposite order
      `Less` and `More` exchange; `Same` is unchanged.
  entry #sign   `-1`, `0` or `1`: the `Comparator`/`Ordering` convention, for stdlib and JDK seams
  entry #also   lexicographic composition: this unless it is `Same`, else the next comparison
      The second comparison is by-name, so a chain of `also`s stops at the first decisive
      result and evaluates nothing after it.

entry .Populated   a proof of non-emptiness in a value's type: `List[Int] & Populated`
    A phantom structural refinement, `Any { type Occupied = true }`, intersected with a
    collection type to record that it has at least one element. The proof is minted two
    ways: the fixed-arity literal constructors (`List(1, 2, 3)`, `Sequence(t"a")`) brand
    their result by construction, and rudiments' `occupied` checks a value once and yields
    `Optional[self & Populated]`. The operations which are only total on a non-empty
    receiver — `head`, `last`, `tail`, `reduce` — then demand a `Populated` receiver, or
    degrade to an `Optional` result, so partiality is discharged where the code compiles.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val proven = List(4, 5, 6)         // List[Int] & Populated
    proven.head                        // 4: an Int, not an Optional[Int]
    List.empty[Int].occupied           // Unset
    List(7, 8).occupied.let(_.head)    // 7
    ```

    A refinement rather than a trait so that it erases away: `Text & Populated` is still
    a `String` at runtime, where a nominal marker would insert casts that fail. The
    brand is lost by any transformation and by a type ascription to the plain type. It
    lives here, below every collection, so the companions can brand their literals;
    `rudiments.Populated` is an alias of it.

entry .Sized   a proof of length in a value's type: `Array[Byte]^{} & Sized[512]`
    The counterpart of `Populated` for fixed-layout data — a tar header of 512 bytes, a
    compass rose of eight points. `Sized[length]` is the refinement `Any { type Length =
    length }`, minted three ways: `Array.fill` and `Array.tabulate` (and `Data.fill`) with
    a *literal* count brand their result by construction; a producer which fixes a layout
    declares the brand on its result; and concordance's `sized[n]` checks a value's length
    once and records it, as `occupied` records non-emptiness. A literal ordinal below the
    length is then confined by construction: an `apply` on a `Sized[n]` receiver with a
    literal ordinal decides the bound at compile time, so the read is total and an
    out-of-range literal is a compile error. The length is a type-level `Int`, so
    `scala.compiletime.ops.int` combines proofs: `Array.concat` of two sized arrays is
    `Sized[left + right]`.

entry .Unit   the `Unit` type, as an alias of `scala.Unit`
    `Unit` cannot be `export`ed (its companion is forbidden in source), so the prelude
    declares `type Unit = scala.Unit`, which is a *distinct symbol*. Code is unaffected,
    but a macro which compares `TypeRepr.of[Unit].typeSymbol` with a method's return type
    symbol finds them unequal; compare with `=:=` or after `.dealias`.

entry .classOf   the class of a type, correct through an export: `classOf[Text]`
    Predef's `classOf` is a compiler intrinsic only when its call carries a concrete class
    type, so through an export forwarder every call yielded `Class[Object]`. This inline
    version summons the `ClassTag` at each call site, where the type argument is concrete,
    and takes `runtimeClass` from it.

entry .absolve   `Predef.runtimeChecked`, renamed: marks a match as deliberately non-exhaustive
    `value.absolve match …` tells the compiler the match need not be exhaustive, in place
    of an `@unchecked` annotation. Used in macros which match on a shape they know.

entry .Nat   `Int & Singleton`: the type of a literal natural number
    A bound for a type-level integer parameter, such as a unit's power in quantitative
    (`Units[power <: Nat, …]`).

entry .Label   `String & Singleton`: the type of a string literal
    A bound for a type parameter which must be a string literal, such as a field name, a
    tag name or a property name (`Typeface of "sans-serif"`, `AnnotatedField[…, target <:
    Label]`).

entry .Zero   `EmptyTuple`, renamed: the end of a tuple
    `Zero` is `scala.EmptyTuple`; a one-element tuple is `Mono[value]`, `value *: Zero`.

entry .Mono   a one-element tuple: `Mono[value]` is `value *: Zero`, and `Mono(x)` makes one
    The name for the degenerate tuple which generic tuple code produces, with a
    constructor `Mono(value)`.

entry .~>   `domain ~> range`: an infix alias of `PartialFunction[domain, range]`
    Lets a partial-function parameter read as an arrow: `lambda: (Optional[focus],
    accrual) => Exception ~> accrual`.

entry .prohibits   `result prohibits prohibited`: a given which is selected only if no `prohibited` given exists
    `prohibits[result, prohibited]` is `NotGiven[prohibited] ?-> result`, a pure context
    function: a given of this type is selected for `result` and, on selection, resolves
    its `NotGiven[prohibited]` at the use site, so `given choice: Choice prohibits Policy =
    …` reads as the guard it is. The arrow is pure so that an ordinary object can hold the
    given as a field.

entry .unexported   marks a top-level definition deliberately absent from the `soundness` umbrella
    An annotation for a module, extension method or helper whose simple name would clash
    with another library's in the umbrella package, or which is a compile-time or internal
    helper reached through its own library's import. The export-generating tooling reads
    it and skips the annotated definition.

entry .infer   `infer[context]`: summons a given, inlined
    `transparent inline def infer[context] = summonInline[context]`: the house spelling of
    `summonInline`, used where an instance is summoned inside an inline method so that the
    search happens at the expansion site. `summon` is the non-inline form.

entry .provide   runs a block with a given of a named type supplied from the call site
    `provide[Context](block)` summons a `Context` where it is expanded and passes it to the
    context-function `block`, so that a method which needs a `Tactic[Error]` can be
    called from inline code which has one: `provide[Tactic[Pojo.Error]]: …`. The `Void`
    parameter is a placeholder which lets the result type parameter be inferred after
    the context one is given.

entry .->   `left -> right`: the pair `(left, right)`
    The tuple arrow, supplied here because `scala.Predef` made it an inline method which
    `-Yno-predef` would otherwise lose. `Map(t"a" -> 1)` depends on it.

entry .Void   `scala.DummyImplicit`, renamed: a placeholder using-parameter
    A `(using erased void: Void)` parameter lets a method separate a type parameter the
    caller gives from one the compiler infers: `safely[error](using erased void:
    Void)[success](…)`. Also exported under its original name, `DummyImplicit`.

entry .is   `subject is Typeclass`: refines a typeclass's `Self` to its subject
    Predef's infix `is`, re-exported: `Text is Showable` is `Showable { type Self = Text }`,
    the phrase every Soundness typeclass is summoned and declared by, and the root the
    prepositional prepositions (`in`, `by`, `to`, …) extend.

entry .nn   asserts a nullable value is not `null`: `javaResult.nn`
    Predef's `nn`, for a `T | Null` from a Java API whose documentation promises a
    value; throws `NullPointerException` if the promise is broken. Where `null` is a
    legitimate outcome, use `Optional(value)` instead.

entry .summon   `summon[T]`: the given of a type, from Predef
    The non-inline summon; `infer[T]` is the inline form for use inside inline methods.

entry .identity   `identity(x)` is `x`, from Predef

entry .locally   `locally { … }`: a block as an expression, from Predef
    Chiefly for a block which opens with a `given` import whose scope should end with it,
    such as a choice between `alphabets` or `dysasymptotics` givens.

entry .valueOf   `valueOf[literal]`: the value of a singleton type, from Predef

entry .assert   Predef's `assert(condition)` and `assert(condition, message)`

entry .???   Predef's `???`: a placeholder which throws `NotImplementedError`

entry .ArrowAssoc   Predef's `ArrowAssoc`: the class behind `->`
    Kept so that stdlib code and the `->` extension here agree; the pair arrow itself is
    the `->` extension.

entry .$conforms   Predef's `$conforms`: the given behind every `A <:< A` evidence
  hidden
  detail
      Re-exported from Predef so that subtype evidence (`<:<`) still resolves under
      `-Yno-predef`; not for direct use.

entry .augmentString   Predef's `String` to `StringOps` conversion, for the few `String` seams

entry .intWrapper   Predef's `Int` to `RichInt`, for `until`, `to`, `max`, `min` on `Int`
entry .longWrapper   Predef's `Long` to `RichLong`
entry .shortWrapper   Predef's `Short` to `RichShort`
entry .byteWrapper   Predef's `Byte` to `RichByte`
entry .charWrapper   Predef's `Char` to `RichChar`, for `isDigit`, `toUpper` and the like
entry .doubleWrapper   Predef's `Double` to `RichDouble`
entry .floatWrapper   Predef's `Float` to `RichFloat`

entry .refArrayOps   Predef's `ArrayOps` conversion for a `scala.Array` of references
    The array-ops conversions apply to the raw `scala.Array` at a JDK seam, not to the
    opaque `Array`, whose vocabulary is the typeclass one.
entry .genericArrayOps   Predef's `ArrayOps` conversion for a `scala.Array` of any element
entry .byteArrayOps   Predef's `ArrayOps` conversion for a `scala.Array[Byte]`
entry .shortArrayOps   Predef's `ArrayOps` conversion for a `scala.Array[Short]`
entry .intArrayOps   Predef's `ArrayOps` conversion for a `scala.Array[Int]`
entry .longArrayOps   Predef's `ArrayOps` conversion for a `scala.Array[Long]`
entry .floatArrayOps   Predef's `ArrayOps` conversion for a `scala.Array[Float]`
entry .doubleArrayOps   Predef's `ArrayOps` conversion for a `scala.Array[Double]`
entry .charArrayOps   Predef's `ArrayOps` conversion for a `scala.Array[Char]`
entry .booleanArrayOps   Predef's `ArrayOps` conversion for a `scala.Array[Boolean]`
entry .unitArrayOps   Predef's `ArrayOps` conversion for a `scala.Array[Unit]`

entry .ClassTag   `scala.reflect.ClassTag`, re-exported: the runtime class token an array element needs
    Every `Array` constructor takes a `ClassTag[element]` context bound, so a generic
    method which builds arrays carries `[element: ClassTag]`.

entry .Typeable   `scala.reflect.Typeable`, re-exported: a runtime type test for a type pattern

entry .TreeMap   `scala.collection.immutable.TreeMap`, re-exported: a sorted stdlib map
    A stdlib structure kept for the few places which need key order, at the cost of an
    explicit `import scala.math.Ordering` for its keys. Not an opaque Soundness collection.

entry .TreeSet   `scala.collection.immutable.TreeSet`, re-exported: a sorted stdlib set

entry .TrieMap   `scala.collection.concurrent.TrieMap`, re-exported: a concurrent stdlib map

entry .NonFatal   `scala.util.control.NonFatal`, re-exported: the extractor for recoverable throwables

entry .boundary   `scala.util.boundary`, re-exported: a labelled block to `break` out of
entry .break   `scala.util.boundary.break`, re-exported: leaves the enclosing `boundary` with a value

entry .IteratorHasAsScala   `.asScala` on a `java.util.Iterator`, from `CollectionConverters`
    The Java-interop converters are re-exported so that a JDK seam needs no extra import;
    the result is a stdlib collection, crossed back with the companion's `from`.
entry .ListHasAsScala   `.asScala` on a `java.util.List`, from `CollectionConverters`
entry .MapHasAsScala   `.asScala` on a `java.util.Map`, from `CollectionConverters`
entry .EnumerationHasAsScala   `.asScala` on a `java.util.Enumeration`, from `CollectionConverters`
entry .SeqHasAsJava   `.asJava` on a stdlib `Seq`, from `CollectionConverters`
entry .MapHasAsJava   `.asJava` on a stdlib `Map`, from `CollectionConverters`

entry .tailrec   `scala.annotation.tailrec`, re-exported
entry .targetName   `scala.annotation.targetName`, re-exported
entry .switch   `scala.annotation.switch`, re-exported
entry .StaticAnnotation   `scala.annotation.StaticAnnotation`, re-exported: the base of a user annotation
entry .missingContext   `scala.annotation.implicitNotFound`, renamed: the message for a missing given
    `@missingContext("…")` on a typeclass or a given's type sets the error reported when
    no instance is found.
entry .uncheckedVariance   `scala.annotation.unchecked.uncheckedVariance`, re-exported
entry .uncheckedCaptures   `scala.annotation.unchecked.uncheckedCaptures`, re-exported
entry .uncheckedStable   `scala.annotation.unchecked.uncheckedStable`, re-exported

entry .into   `Conversion.into`, re-exported: marks a parameter as accepting implicit conversions

entry .Any   `scala.Any`, re-exported
entry .AnyKind   `scala.AnyKind`, re-exported: the bound admitting a type of any kind
entry .AnyRef   `scala.AnyRef`, re-exported
entry .AnyVal   `scala.AnyVal`, re-exported
entry .Matchable   `scala.Matchable`, re-exported
entry .Nothing   `scala.Nothing`, re-exported
    The type of an expression which never yields — `panic`, `abort` and a tactic's
    `raise` return it — so it conforms to every type.
entry .Null   `scala.Null`, re-exported
    The type of `null` under explicit nulls; a Java API's nullable `T | Null` is made
    an `Optional` with `Optional(value)` or asserted with `nn`.
entry .Singleton   `scala.Singleton`, re-exported: the bound for a literal type parameter
entry .&   the infix intersection type, `A & B`, re-exported
entry .|   the infix union type, `A | B`, re-exported
entry .<:<   the subtype evidence `A <:< B`, re-exported
entry .=:=   the type-equality evidence `A =:= B`, re-exported
entry .Boolean   `scala.Boolean`, re-exported
entry .Byte   `scala.Byte`, re-exported
entry .Char   `scala.Char`, re-exported
entry .Double   `scala.Double`, re-exported
entry .Float   `scala.Float`, re-exported
entry .Int   `scala.Int`, re-exported
entry .Long   `scala.Long`, re-exported
entry .Short   `scala.Short`, re-exported
entry .Either   `scala.Either`, re-exported
entry .Left   `scala.Left`, re-exported
entry .Right   `scala.Right`, re-exported
entry .Option   `scala.Option`, re-exported, for the boundary with code which uses it
    Soundness code represents absence as an `Optional`; `Option` is kept for stdlib and
    third-party APIs, converted at the seam with `optional` and `option`.
entry .Some   `scala.Some`, re-exported
entry .None   `scala.None`, re-exported
entry .Tuple   `scala.Tuple`, re-exported
entry .EmptyTuple   `scala.EmptyTuple`, re-exported; also available as `Zero`
entry .NonEmptyTuple   `scala.NonEmptyTuple`, re-exported
entry .Tuple1   `scala.Tuple1`, re-exported
entry .Tuple2   `scala.Tuple2`, re-exported
entry .*:   the tuple cons type and extractor `head *: tail`, re-exported
entry .CanEqual   `scala.CanEqual`, re-exported: strict-equality evidence
entry .Conversion   `scala.Conversion`, re-exported: the class of an implicit conversion
entry .Dynamic   `scala.Dynamic`, re-exported: the marker for `selectDynamic` members
entry .Function   `scala.Function`, re-exported
entry .PartialFunction   `scala.PartialFunction`, re-exported; also written infix as `~>`
entry .Product   `scala.Product`, re-exported
entry .Selectable   `scala.Selectable`, re-exported: structural selection
entry .StringContext   `scala.StringContext`, re-exported: what an interpolator extends
entry .BigDecimal   `scala.BigDecimal`, re-exported
entry .BigInt   `scala.BigInt`, re-exported
entry .Fractional   `scala.Fractional`, re-exported
entry .Integral   `scala.Integral`, re-exported
entry .Numeric   `scala.Numeric`, re-exported
    The numeric abstractions are kept for the stdlib seams which take them; `Ordering`
    and `Ordered` are deliberately not, since comparison in Soundness is `Comparable`.
entry .deprecated   `scala.deprecated`, re-exported
entry .unchecked   `scala.unchecked`, re-exported
entry .volatile   `scala.volatile`, re-exported
entry .transient   `scala.transient`, re-exported
entry .native   `scala.native`, re-exported
entry .main   `scala.main`, re-exported: the annotation of a program's entry method
entry .unsafeExceptions   `scala.unsafeExceptions`, re-exported: `import unsafeExceptions.canThrowAny`
    The object whose `canThrowAny` given lets any exception be thrown without a
    `CanThrow`, for code which has decided not to track them.
entry .CanThrow   `scala.CanThrow`, re-exported: the capability to throw an exception type
entry .DummyImplicit   `scala.DummyImplicit`, re-exported; also available as `Void`
entry .MatchError   `scala.MatchError`, re-exported
entry .NamedTuple   `scala.NamedTuple`, re-exported
entry .Specializable   `scala.Specializable`, re-exported
entry .ValueOf   `scala.ValueOf`, re-exported: the value of a singleton type, as evidence
entry .NoSuchElementException   `scala.NoSuchElementException`, re-exported
entry .Iterable   `scala.Iterable`, re-exported: the one stdlib collection interface kept
    The abstract interface is the internal currency of the collection typeclasses
    (`compact`, `entire` and the `Iterable` receivers in rudiments take one); concrete
    stdlib collections are not re-exported. `Iterator` is *not* here: under `import
    soundness.*` it is murmuration's.
entry .StringBuilder   `scala.StringBuilder`, re-exported, for now, as the mutable text accumulator
    Marked temporary in the prelude, pending a review of mutable text accumulation.
